 the race a Republican flip, with a few rating it a tossup, and prediction markets give Soto roughly a three-in-ten chance.\n\nSoto retains real strengths: he has outraised Green substantially, holds a large cash-on-hand edge, and has launched bipartisan and Spanish-language advertising while contrasting his Social Security record with Green's support for spending cuts. A single August poll showed the candidates tied, with many voters still undecided. The fundamentals and ratings point to a narrow Republican edge, but the race remains competitive enough that late campaign dynamics could matter.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.3442, "margin": -2.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2846, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2806, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2308, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3096, "margin": -2.6}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3559, "margin": -2.6}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.3413, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3329, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2822, "margin": -4.0}]}